<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Selecting intermediate features from a frozen diffusion model for
discriminative tasks is non-trivial because the useful signal is distributed
across the denoising trajectory, network depth, and channels. In practice, this
requires choosing a timestep, a block, and a channel subset. Existing methods
either fix these choices by hand or optimize only a few extraction
hyperparameters, most often the timestep, then reuse the same rule across
inputs and budgets. We introduce <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Policy-Mask</span>, a budget-conditioned policy
trained on the downstream objective that jointly selects all three under a
prescribed channel budget. Without normalization, the channel-score
contribution grows with the number of selected channels and dominates the
timestep and block updates. We normalize it by subset cardinality, keeping the
three policy heads on comparable scales and stabilizing joint training. At
matched budgets, <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Policy-Mask</span> improves over fixed extraction and feature-filter
baselines across segmentation, classification, and semantic correspondence,
reaching 38.9 mIoU on ADE20K, 75.4% top-1 on CIFAR-100 at 128 dimensions, and
76.3 PCK@0.1 on SPair-71k. Component-wise ablations show that block selection
materially affects downstream accuracy, and the highest recorded channel-budget
score occurs at 256 retained channels, identifying a practical operating point
for compact diffusion representations. We further adapt <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Policy-Mask</span> to
diffusion transformers, including PixArt-<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and SD3.5 MMDiT, by
replacing U-Net channels with image-token hidden dimensions.</p>
  
</div>
