<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">At 128 dimensions, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Policy-Mask</span> reaches 95.2% top-1 on CIFAR-10 and 75.4%
on CIFAR-100, 1.0 and 1.3 points above the reported RepFusion entries
(Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.2 Main results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Policy-Mask: Budget-Aware Adaptation of Diffusion Features to Discriminative Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). On SPair-71k, it reaches 76.3 PCK@0.1 with
image normalization and 67.5 with bounding-box normalization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.4 Transformer teachers ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Policy-Mask: Budget-Aware Adaptation of Diffusion Features to Discriminative Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows corrected facade labels and fewer false
regions in cemetery, bakery, and sea scenes among the outcome-selected examples.
The corresponding losses and class-wise differences are reported in
Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Qualitative and class-wise analysis ‣ Policy-Mask: Budget-Aware Adaptation of Diffusion Features to Discriminative Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Ablation study</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Action axes.</span>
In the controlled action-axis ablation, expanding a
timestep-only policy to the time–layer grid raises mIoU from 31.78 to 33.32.
Adding a heuristic top-<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> subset reaches 32.82, while the unnormalized joint
policy reaches 31.68 (Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.4 Transformer teachers ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Policy-Mask: Budget-Aware Adaptation of Diffusion Features to Discriminative Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). Expanding the action
space alone therefore does not improve accuracy in this setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Channel-score normalization.</span>
With <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> fixed, <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Policy-Mask</span> reaches <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="37.7\pm 0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>37.7</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">37.7\pm 0.7</annotation></semantics></math> mIoU, compared with
<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="36.8\pm 0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>36.8</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">36.8\pm 0.6</annotation></semantics></math> without the <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/b</annotation></semantics></math> channel-score factor
(Table <a href="#A2.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ Policy-Mask: Budget-Aware Adaptation of Diffusion Features to Discriminative Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). Both use the same three seeds and training
settings. This comparison is separate from the single-seed action-axis
ablation and the main segmentation table.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Channel budget.</span>
The sweep in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.4 Transformer teachers ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Policy-Mask: Budget-Aware Adaptation of Diffusion Features to Discriminative Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> uses top-magnitude selection at a
fixed timestep and layer, with a separate student trained for each budget.
It does not use a learned policy. Increasing <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> from 128 to 256 raises the
best validation mIoU by 0.72 and the mean over the last five epochs by 0.27.
The sweep changes both the selected features and the KD-loss scale, so it
does not identify an optimal budget independently of distillation strength
(Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Channel-budget sweep ‣ Appendix A Experimental details ‣ Policy-Mask: Budget-Aware Adaptation of Diffusion Features to Discriminative Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>). We retain <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=128</annotation></semantics></math> for the compact representation
used in the selector comparisons.</p>
</div>
</section>
