<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Limitations</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Channel budget.</span> The budget sweep in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.4 Transformer teachers ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Policy-Mask: Budget-Aware Adaptation of Diffusion Features to Discriminative Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>
changes the selected features and the distillation strength together. The
masked KD loss is averaged over all 1280 channels, so its mean over epochs
10–19 grows from 0.59 at <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=128</annotation></semantics></math> to 0.94 at <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=256</annotation></semantics></math> and 1.35 at <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=512</annotation></semantics></math>. The
drop at <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=512</annotation></semantics></math> therefore cannot be attributed to redundant channels.
A follow-up sweep with KD normalized over the selected channels would control
this change in loss scale
(Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Channel-budget sweep ‣ Appendix A Experimental details ‣ Policy-Mask: Budget-Aware Adaptation of Diffusion Features to Discriminative Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Repetitions.</span> The normalization ablation and the RepFusion
comparison are repeated over three seeds (Table <a href="#A2.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ Policy-Mask: Budget-Aware Adaptation of Diffusion Features to Discriminative Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).
The action-axis ablation, the budget sweep, and the qualitative analysis use a
single seed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Transformer teachers.</span> Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.4 Transformer teachers ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Policy-Mask: Budget-Aware Adaptation of Diffusion Features to Discriminative Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> matches
the capacity fraction across teachers but uses one evaluation per teacher. It
supports the transferred action interface, not a ranking of teacher
architectures.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We presented <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Policy-Mask</span>, which treats diffusion feature extraction as a
budgeted decision: a policy jointly selects the timestep, the layer, and an
exact-size channel subset of a frozen teacher, and the selected features
distill a student that runs alone at inference. The experiments support three
conclusions. First, joint selection pays off at a fixed budget: with 128 of
1280 channels, <span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Policy-Mask</span> exceeds full-width extraction at a fixed
extraction point on all four segmentation benchmarks and exceeds RepFusion on
CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100. Second, channel-score normalization improves the
tested joint policy. Without the <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/b</annotation></semantics></math> factor, the joint policy falls below the
timestep-only policy in the action-axis ablation. With fixed alignment,
normalization raises
ADE20K mIoU from 36.8 to 37.7 over three seeds. Third, the action transfers
to DiT and MMDiT teachers by replacing U-Net channels with image-token hidden
dimensions while retaining the student loss and exact-cardinality constraint. Separating
channel count from distillation strength remains the main open question for
choosing the operating budget.</p>
</div>
</section>
