<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Fixed-slot reference runs</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Appendix B Additional results ‣ Policy-Mask: Budget-Aware Adaptation of Diffusion Features to Discriminative Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> compares feature selection methods on ADE20K
at a fixed timestep and layer. The full-width baseline uses 1280 channels,
while random and top-variance selection retain 128 channels.
These 14-epoch reference runs follow a separate protocol and are not compared
numerically with Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.2 Main results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Policy-Mask: Budget-Aware Adaptation of Diffusion Features to Discriminative Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Channel-score normalization and RepFusion comparison</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The normalized and unnormalized policies use fixed <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, the same seeds,
and the same training settings. They differ only in the <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/b</annotation></semantics></math> channel-score
factor. Our ADE20K implementation of RepFusion uses SD-1.5 and follows two
stages: feature distillation followed by segmentation training. The
teacher-side alignment remains fixed. RepFusion uses all 1280 dimensions,
while <span id="A2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Policy-Mask</span> retains 128.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Qualitative and class-wise analysis</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The seed-0 comparison covers 2000 ADE20K validation images. <span id="A3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Policy-Mask</span>
has higher image-mIoU than RepFusion on 39.1% of images. This image-level
comparison is separate from the dataset mIoU reported across three seeds.
Class IoU improves for ball, flag, bridge, sand, monitor, and mountain,
with losses for sconce, sink, animal, and desk (Figure <a href="#A3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Appendix C Qualitative and class-wise analysis ‣ Policy-Mask: Budget-Aware Adaptation of Diffusion Features to Discriminative Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).
The gain and loss panels show the 12 largest differences in each direction.</p>
</div>
